    {
      "teach": "**str_detect(text, \"pattern\")** gives TRUE if the text contains the pattern. It works great inside filter(): `filter(str_detect(email, \"wes.edu\"))`.",
      "prompt": "How many club sign-ups are for chess? The capitals are messy, so make the club names lowercase first, then use str_detect(): n_chess <- sum(str_detect(str_to_lower(club_signups$club), \"chess\")).",
      "starter_code": "",
      "check": {
        "type": "value",
        "object": "n_chess",
        "expected": 3,
        "must_use": [
          "str_detect"
        ]
      },
      "hints": [
        "str_detect(text, pattern) gives TRUE or FALSE for each value. Lowercase the text first so \"Chess\" and \"chess\" both match.",
        "n_chess <- sum(str_detect(str_to_lower(club_signups$club), \"chess\"))"
      ],
      "answer": "n_chess <- sum(str_detect(str_to_lower(club_signups$club), \"chess\"))"
    },
    {
      "teach": "In a pattern, **`.*`** means \"anything, any length\". So `\"@.*\"` matches the @ and everything after it. **str_replace(text, pattern, new)** swaps it for something instead of deleting it.",
      "prompt": "Last one: get each person's username by removing everything from the @ onwards: usernames <- str_remove(club_signups$email, \"@.*\").",
      "starter_code": "",
      "check": {
        "type": "value",
        "object": "usernames",
        "expected": [
          "maya",
          "leo",
          "sam",
          "ava",
          "leo",
          "noah",
          "zoe",
          "ava"
        ],
        "must_use": [
          "str_remove"
        ]
      },
      "hints": [
        "str_remove(text, pattern) deletes the matching part.",
        "usernames <- str_remove(club_signups$email, \"@.*\")"
      ],
      "answer": "usernames <- str_remove(club_signups$email, \"@.*\")"
    }
  ],
  "recap": "str_detect() finds text containing a pattern, str_remove() and str_replace() delete or swap it, and parse_number() pulls numbers out of text like \"$40\"."
